  <h1>IVD OEM vs ODM</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> In <strong>OEM</strong> (original equipment manufacturing), you provide the specification — analyte, cut-off, format, packaging — and the manufacturer builds to it. In <strong>ODM</strong> (original design manufacturing), the manufacturer already has the design and you buy it under your brand. OEM is "build to my spec"; ODM is "rebrand an existing product".</p>

  <h2>The difference</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>OEM</th><th>ODM</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Design</th><td>Yours (you specify)</td><td>Manufacturer's (existing)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Customization</th><td>High — analyte, cut-off, format, packaging</td><td>Low — branding, label, packaging</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Lead time</th><td>Longer (validation required)</td><td>Shorter</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Best for</th><td>A unique spec or private recipe</td><td>A proven product, fast to market</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>When to choose each</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Choose OEM</strong> when you need a specific cut-off, a custom panel, or a differentiation that an off-the-shelf product does not have — and you accept the validation lead time.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose ODM</strong> when you want a proven, registered product fast under your own brand, without the development and validation cycle.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to watch</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>OEM customization must be <strong>re-validated</strong> — changing a cut-off or format changes performance.</li>
    <li>Confirm who holds the <strong>regulatory file</strong> — the manufacturer, the buyer, or a shared arrangement.</li>
    <li>Confirm the <strong>minimum order quantity (MOQ)</strong> and lead time before committing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
  <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, most first-time IVD importers overpay for OEM when ODM would serve — unless the cut-off or panel is genuinely your differentiator, buy the proven design and spend the difference on branding.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> OEM buys you a unique specification, but it also buys you a validation cycle and a longer lead time. ODM gets you to market fast with a product that is already registered and stable. The right answer is rarely "always OEM" — it is "what is actually different about my product".</p>
  <p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as an IVD manufacturer's technical team, not a contractual statement.</p>

